    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by identifying the greatest common factor (GCF) of the terms in the expression $9x^2+3x$. The GCF of $9x^2$ and $3x$ is $3x$. We then factor out the GCF:\n\n\\[9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1) = 3x(3x + 1)\\]\n\nThus, the completely factored form of the expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\n\\] To completely factor the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\), we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the greatest common factor (GCF):**\n   - The coefficients of the terms are \\(9\\) and \\(3\\). The GCF of \\(9\\) and \\(3\\) is \\(3\\).\n   - The variables in the terms are \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\). The GCF of \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\) is \\(x\\).\n   - Therefore, the GCF of the entire expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is \\(3x\\).\n\n2. **Factor out the GCF:**\n   - We divide each term in the expression by the GCF \\(3x\\):\n     \\[\n     9x^2 \\div 3x = 3x\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3x \\div 3x = 1\n     \\]\n   - So, we can write:\n     \\[\n     9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1) = 3x(3x + 1)\n     \\]\n\n3. **Write the final factored form:**\n   - The completely factored form of the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\\).",
